  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 251 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1:  High pressure will continue to remain across the region \ntoday, bringing another day of tranquil weather after any early \nmorning fog dissipates. High temperatures this afternoon will warm \ninto the upper 60s to low 70s, with some increasing high clouds \nexpected as the day progresses ahead of the next approaching system, \nlimiting any fog or frost concerns for tonight.\n\nA Nor'easter will continue to track along the coast, bringing heavy \nrainfall and strong winds to portions of the mid-Atlantic and New \nEngland. Our region will be on the periphery of the system, with the \ngreatest impacts expected outside of our area. The better chances \nfor precipitation will be further south, although there is still \nsome uncertainty as the to the overall extent of showers Saturday \ninto Sunday. In addition to the precipitation chances, a strong jet \nassociated with the system will bring gusty surface winds, \nespecially across the higher terrain and the western slopes. \nTemperatures will be on the cooler side as well, especially Sunday \nas clouds and periods of showers are expected, especially across \nsouthern and eastern Vermont.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: As the system slowly shifts eastward, shower chances \nwill linger into the start of next week, gradually diminishing \ntowards Tuesday. A frontal boundary looks to move through towards \nmid-week, bringing some additional chances for showers to the \nregion, although there is still plenty of uncertainty at this time \nwith a wide spread amongst models towards that time frame. \nTemperatures will be on the cooler side to start the week, \ngenerally in the 60s areawide, gradually warming a few degrees \ntowards the middle of the week with highs in the upper 60s and lower \n70s. Lingering showers and cloud cover will prevent overnight \ntemperatures from radiating out, with temperatures most \nlikely dropping into the 40s and 50s.",
